#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161907 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162094 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f930dfb2-a688-5dbe-9233-09dbcd2ba383" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sample Mean of an Observation Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "5f949e10-0d73-50c8-9f41-73a0d0f78e29" :question/math-academy-id "e-9833" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample mean vector of $X$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the sample mean vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{m} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\mathbf{x}_i\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{x}_n$ are the individual observations (columns of $X$).\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{1}{4}(x_{1} + x_{2} + x_{3} + x_{4}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161908 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162095 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc84d72a-b9f3-5f99-aa23-31cfe6910d49" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Covariance Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "bd9fe016-cd5f-5e65-b388-35725ac0fbf4" :question/math-academy-id "e-9834" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample covariance matrix of the data." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} B B^T\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations.\n\nSo, we first need to compute the mean-deviation form of our observation matrix.\n\nThe sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{1}{3}(x_{1} + x_{2} + x_{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb_{1} &= x_{1} - m = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nb_{2} &= x_{2} - m = \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nb_{3} &= x_{3} - m = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample covariance matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC &= \\frac{1}{3 - 1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 50\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161909 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162096 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e91708c9-67e2-5228-84fd-1f4ea60064ac" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mean-Deviation Form of an Observation Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "bdef2fdc-0a38-5bfa-9bc6-7ca50e8c10ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-9835" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 9 & 4 \\\\ 15 & -7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the mean-deviation form of $X$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the mean-deviation form of the observation matrix\n\n$$\n[ \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid; x_{1}\\begin{vmatrix}x_{2} & ⋯\\end{vmatrix}x_{n}; \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid ]\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n[ \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid; b_{1}\\begin{vmatrix}b_{2} & ⋯\\end{vmatrix}b_{n}; \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid ]\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b}_i = \\mathbf{x}_i - \\mathbf{m}\n$$\n\nfor\n\n$$\ni = 1,2, \\ldots, n\n$$\n\nand $\\mathbf{m}$ is sample mean vector.\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{1}{3}(x_{1} + x_{2} + x_{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb_{1} &= x_{1} - m = \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nb_{2} &= x_{2} - m = \\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nb_{3} &= x_{3} - m = \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 12 & -10 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162353 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162096 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e91708c9-67e2-5228-84fd-1f4ea60064ac" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mean-Deviation Form of an Observation Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d46b5f4-ced0-5d28-add8-69a0fba6650c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17